WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.588 --> 00:00:10.799
I'm going to start with a story.

00:00:11.383 --> 00:00:14.511
In 1730, in India,

00:00:14.511 --> 00:00:18.348
the king of Jodhpur
wanted to build a new palace,

00:00:18.348 --> 00:00:21.393
and he needed a large number
of trees to be cut.

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:24.980
Alarmed at hearing this,
a group of local environmental activists

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.150
went to protect the trees
by throwing their arms around them.

00:00:29.985 --> 00:00:33.405
We could consider them
the first-ever tree huggers.

00:00:34.573 --> 00:00:37.158
Now, while this story
might be known to many of us,

00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:40.829
what's often less known
is what motivated these activists.

00:00:41.454 --> 00:00:44.541
They came from the Bishnoi community,

00:00:44.541 --> 00:00:50.463
a Hindu lineage founded
on 29 principles of environmental care.

00:00:51.298 --> 00:00:54.593
This is just one of so many
amazing examples,

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:55.760
throughout the world,

00:00:55.802 --> 00:00:59.848
of religious communities stepping up
to protect the environment

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.892
through addressing climate change,

00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:03.894
restoring ecosystems

00:01:03.935 --> 00:01:05.812
and protecting biodiversity.

00:01:06.771 --> 00:01:09.274
And I've had the honor and the privilege

00:01:09.316 --> 00:01:12.986
of working with many such groups
in different parts of the world

00:01:13.028 --> 00:01:14.696
for environmental action.

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:18.783
But before I did that,
I actually was living in India,

00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:23.955
where I spent time in a Hindu ashram
on the banks of the sacred river Ganges,

00:01:23.997 --> 00:01:26.583
where I was studying Hindu spirituality

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:29.502
and how the teachings of the Bhagavad Gita

00:01:29.544 --> 00:01:33.340
could be applied to contemporary
concerns in the world.

00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:40.221
And it was this orientation
of an engaged religion and spirituality

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:43.767
that brought me
to the climate movement in 2010.

00:01:44.476 --> 00:01:47.812
And since then, I think like many of us,

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:51.274
I have experienced the highs and the lows

00:01:51.274 --> 00:01:53.902
as we've campaigned and advocated

00:01:53.902 --> 00:01:57.739
to keep the global temperature
increase to 1.5 degrees.

00:01:58.323 --> 00:01:59.741
And I don't know about you,

00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:03.078
but I think right now,
it can feel like it's a difficult time

00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:05.163
to be working on climate change.

00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:09.542
Sometimes, things just don't seem
to be moving as quickly as we would like.

00:02:10.168 --> 00:02:15.674
And more often than not, unfortunately,
we might be feeling scared or stuck,

00:02:15.674 --> 00:02:18.343
hopeless or maybe even burnt out.

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:21.137
Coming from the faith sector,

00:02:21.179 --> 00:02:23.473
I've always been interested in other ideas

00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:26.226
that are rooted in religion
and spirituality

00:02:26.226 --> 00:02:29.312
that can support the broader
environmental movement.

00:02:29.312 --> 00:02:33.358
I started looking into the history
of religious and spiritual groups,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.360
and started thinking that they could be

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.322
some of the most enduring social movements
in the history of the world.

00:02:39.698 --> 00:02:43.243
They've been able to survive
through multiple generations,

00:02:43.243 --> 00:02:45.537
adapt to changing situations,

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:48.206
reform and revive themselves when needed.

00:02:48.248 --> 00:02:49.958
They've even inspired the leaders

00:02:49.958 --> 00:02:53.670
of some of the most iconic
social movements in recent history,

00:02:53.712 --> 00:02:57.298
such as Mahatma Gandhi
and Martin Luther King Jr.

00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:00.552
That's why I was really happy
when a friend of mine,

00:03:00.552 --> 00:03:04.764
who was writing a book
about Hindu approaches to pastoral care,

00:03:04.806 --> 00:03:06.850
asked me to contribute a chapter.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:10.478
Using my previous background
as a university chaplain,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.523
I developed a framework
that I thought could help people

00:03:13.523 --> 00:03:15.525
suffering from climate trauma.

00:03:16.026 --> 00:03:17.193
And as I went around

00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:20.155
sharing this framework with people
from different backgrounds,

00:03:20.196 --> 00:03:22.741
they told me that it really
resonated with them.

00:03:22.782 --> 00:03:26.453
The framework, although initially
built for climate trauma,

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:30.165
I think can really help with something
I believe we need now

00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:31.458
in the climate movement:

00:03:31.499 --> 00:03:34.502
building resiliency and keeping momentum.

00:03:35.712 --> 00:03:40.759
There are three core interconnected
parts to this framework

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.013
that are drawn from across a variety
of religious and spiritual traditions.

00:03:45.055 --> 00:03:46.806
I'm excited to share that with you,

00:03:46.806 --> 00:03:48.725
and I'm going to use the Sanskrit words

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:51.644
to explain the different aspects
of the framework.

00:03:51.686 --> 00:03:54.647
We begin with "sangha,"

00:03:54.689 --> 00:03:58.735
nourishing and uplifting community,

00:03:58.735 --> 00:04:02.113
where people come together
to support one another

00:04:02.113 --> 00:04:04.282
and reaffirm social ties.

00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:09.704
This can happen weekly, monthly,
or at different times of the year.

00:04:09.704 --> 00:04:14.084
So, for example, my Christian friends,
they go to church on a Sunday.

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:17.962
My Muslim friends
go to prayer on a Friday,

00:04:17.962 --> 00:04:19.380
and my Hindu friends,

00:04:19.422 --> 00:04:22.675
they will gather at major
festivals, such as Diwali.

00:04:23.134 --> 00:04:25.595
We then have "sadhana,"

00:04:25.637 --> 00:04:30.225
rituals and traditions that give us
a sense of belonging.

00:04:30.892 --> 00:04:34.521
This can often be fasting
at times of the year,

00:04:34.521 --> 00:04:36.564
or going on pilgrimage,

00:04:36.564 --> 00:04:39.651
or retelling stories
that have been passed down

00:04:39.692 --> 00:04:41.361
for multiple generations.

00:04:41.903 --> 00:04:44.572
Buddhists will go on pilgrimage to Gaya,

00:04:44.614 --> 00:04:47.200
where the Buddha found enlightenment.

00:04:47.242 --> 00:04:52.163
Jewish families and communities
will retell the stories of their ancestors

00:04:52.205 --> 00:04:54.457
when they gather for their holidays.

00:04:55.542 --> 00:04:59.254
And then, we have "seva,"
purposeful action.

00:04:59.254 --> 00:05:04.134
These groups gave guidance and teachings
for how to show up in the world,

00:05:04.134 --> 00:05:07.095
how to care for one another
and the planet.

00:05:07.095 --> 00:05:08.721
In the Jain tradition,

00:05:08.721 --> 00:05:12.976
there is a focus on leading a life
based on nonviolence.

00:05:13.893 --> 00:05:16.062
Members of the Baha'i faith

00:05:16.062 --> 00:05:22.152
are encouraged to be anxiously concerned
with the needs of the world.

00:05:22.152 --> 00:05:24.571
We do these things on a regular basis,

00:05:24.571 --> 00:05:27.365
but there are two ways
that religious and spiritual groups

00:05:27.365 --> 00:05:28.616
engage in these practices

00:05:28.658 --> 00:05:30.493
that set them slightly apart.

00:05:30.535 --> 00:05:32.453
The first is intention.

00:05:33.288 --> 00:05:36.958
When religious and spiritual groups
engage in these practices,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.627
they do them with a sense of importance,

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:45.425
recognize that these are core functions
of what it means to be part of that group.

00:05:45.466 --> 00:05:49.470
Let's illustrate that with an example
from the climate movement.

00:05:50.013 --> 00:05:53.224
Let's look at the Fridays
for Future school strike.

00:05:53.266 --> 00:05:56.811
That was an example
where some real focused intention

00:05:56.811 --> 00:05:59.105
yielded amazing results.

00:05:59.147 --> 00:06:04.110
By performing a very simple activity,
not going to school on Fridays,

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.279
they were able to build a community,

00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:09.699
a simple ritual and a purposeful action.

00:06:10.325 --> 00:06:14.287
And by doing so, they built
resiliency amongst themselves

00:06:14.329 --> 00:06:17.290
and motivated millions of people
around the world,

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:19.000
that change was possible.

00:06:19.584 --> 00:06:23.171
Now, if we bring
these three practices together,

00:06:23.171 --> 00:06:25.632
along with the intention,

00:06:25.673 --> 00:06:28.468
something really magical starts to happen.

00:06:29.135 --> 00:06:31.554
This is the second special feature

00:06:31.596 --> 00:06:34.974
that religious and spiritual groups
bring to these practices.

00:06:35.391 --> 00:06:38.603
It's something that provides
the ultimate grounding

00:06:38.645 --> 00:06:41.564
and narrative and meaning

00:06:41.606 --> 00:06:46.319
for why community, rituals
and action are important.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.114
I call it “the big idea.”

00:06:50.406 --> 00:06:52.659
For religious and spiritual groups,

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:57.121
the big idea is often a sense
of the divine, of God,

00:06:57.163 --> 00:07:01.167
or a metaphysical
understanding of the world,

00:07:01.209 --> 00:07:04.963
or a grand narrative
of a people throughout time.

00:07:05.672 --> 00:07:10.426
Whatever it is, the big idea usually has
some common characteristics to it.

00:07:11.052 --> 00:07:14.347
It's usually something
that has existed before us

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:16.808
and will exist after us.

00:07:17.141 --> 00:07:23.481
The big idea is not limited
to a certain moment, time or place.

00:07:24.565 --> 00:07:26.985
Now I'm getting some
worried looks in the audience.

00:07:27.026 --> 00:07:30.947
Don't worry, I'm not asking all of us
to go out there and start looking for God.

00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:33.366
But I am suggesting that we may benefit

00:07:33.366 --> 00:07:36.911
from being part of something
bigger than ourselves,

00:07:36.953 --> 00:07:39.247
and that can take many different forms.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.709
For many people I meet
in the climate movement,

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:47.422
their big idea is being part of
and connected to nature,

00:07:47.463 --> 00:07:50.675
and appreciating the beauty
of the natural world.

00:07:50.675 --> 00:07:54.387
But it can also be a sense
of connection we feel to our ancestors

00:07:54.429 --> 00:07:57.765
and the responsibility we have
to future generations.

00:07:58.308 --> 00:08:01.060
It can also be a sense of wonder
about the world,

00:08:01.102 --> 00:08:04.022
space and all that exists.

00:08:04.772 --> 00:08:07.942
Or yes, the big idea could be

00:08:07.984 --> 00:08:10.987
a belief in something bigger
than ourselves

00:08:10.987 --> 00:08:13.781
that we can't quite fully understand,

00:08:13.823 --> 00:08:17.076
but the presence of which we feel
from time to time.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.913
The big idea, the intention,

00:08:20.955 --> 00:08:24.542
and these three practices,
when coming together,

00:08:24.542 --> 00:08:26.502
are extremely powerful

00:08:26.544 --> 00:08:29.672
and are bigger
than the sum of their parts.

00:08:30.423 --> 00:08:32.050
For me,

00:08:32.091 --> 00:08:35.636
my big idea is that the Earth
is a goddess.

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:39.515
In Sanskrit, she is called Bhumi.

00:08:40.725 --> 00:08:46.022
She is the life-giving source
that nourishes me and all that exists.

00:08:46.773 --> 00:08:49.192
In order to sustain this idea,

00:08:49.192 --> 00:08:52.153
I make sure I have a community
of people around me

00:08:52.153 --> 00:08:53.821
who believe similarly.

00:08:54.197 --> 00:08:59.035
Every morning, I have a simple ritual
where I offer her my worship,

00:08:59.077 --> 00:09:01.913
thanking her for all that she provides.

00:09:02.288 --> 00:09:05.625
And I try to show up in the world
with meaningful action,

00:09:05.625 --> 00:09:11.214
treating her with the love, respect
and compassion that she deserves.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:15.718
As we stand in this moment
of time together,

00:09:16.969 --> 00:09:20.139
with all of the data
and science before us,

00:09:21.432 --> 00:09:26.437
I believe we need to equip ourselves
with every tool and resource possible.

00:09:28.147 --> 00:09:33.111
My invitation to all of us
is that we all find our big idea

00:09:33.152 --> 00:09:37.865
and create communities,
rituals and practices

00:09:37.865 --> 00:09:40.910
with renewed intention and purpose.

00:09:41.786 --> 00:09:45.331
This framework not only has the potential

00:09:45.373 --> 00:09:49.335
to help us build resiliency and momentum,

00:09:49.377 --> 00:09:52.171
but it can profoundly transform the world.

00:09:52.588 --> 00:09:58.469
It has worked for people and communities
for thousands of years,

00:09:58.469 --> 00:10:01.597
and perhaps it could work for us now.

00:10:02.473 --> 00:10:03.683
Thank you.

00:10:03.683 --> 00:10:08.604
(Cheers and applause)

